  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 249 AM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nDespite what is generally zonal flow aloft, there is a\nsignificant shortwave mid-level trough moving atop the Rockies \nduring this overnight period which will eject into the Northern \nPlains later this morning. This trough will work in conjunction \nwith a surface frontal boundary draped over southern MN \ncurrently to produce a potentially interesting and active \nweather day for this afternoon-evening.\n\nThe trough will shift across the Dakotas during the day today as \nstrong southerly flow advects both higher moisture and warmer \nair into southern MN and western WI. By midday today, the entire\nWFO MPX coverage area will find itself within the warm sector \nahead of a low pressure center developing over eastern SD, with \nits warm front into northern MN and its cold front sagging south\nover central NE/KS. Highs will range through the 60s and into \nthe lower 70s, warmest near and south/east of the MN River while\ndewpoints rise through the 50s, potentially reaching 60. \nFavorable timing for the frontal passage, allowing for peak \nheating with peak instability, will allow for a fairly wide \nswath of rainfall with embedded thunderstorms in western MN \nearly afternoon which will shift eastward through this evening. \nModel soundings continue to show veering winds with height and \nfavorable hodographs, indicating a decent potential for discrete\nsupercells. Though persistent cloud cover may be the limiting \nfactor, there is enough model and analog evidence that a few \ntornadoes are not unreasonable for today in eastern MN into \nwestern WI, particularly for the mid-late afternoon hours. As \nthe event progresses today, MLCAPEs are still progged to climb \nto around 1000 j/kg, which with colder air aloft, could \ntransition the hazard from tornadoes to a hail/wind threat. Then\nagain, this is also a situation where the ingredients simply do\nnot come together due to lower instability, insufficient \nheating, poor frontal timing, or other meteorological \nhindrances. Thus, today is a highly conditional threat but one \nthat bears watching, especially under the context that collab \nwith the SPC has nudged the risk into the Slight (Level 2 of 5) \nCategory for a small area around the Twin Cities metro and into \nfar western WI.\n\nThe cold front will push well into WI later this evening and\ninto the Great Lakes by daybreak Thursday. As such, any and \nall rainfall will be completely out of our coverage area prior \nto midnight tonight, followed by drier, cooler and much more \nstable high pressure for Thursday. There's even a pretty good \nchance that we'll have plentiful sunshine for Thursday in the \nwake of today's unsettled weather. Highs for Thursday will drop \nto the 50s to lower 60s. The western extent of today's passing \ncold front, located over the Central Plains, will push northward\na bit on Friday into Saturday but not as much as previous \nforecasts. Thus, the chances for rain on Saturday are a bit \nlower and more confined to southern MN for only scattered rain \nshowers.\n\nA better chance for rain still looks to come Monday into Tuesday\nbut now the track of this system looks to stay farther south, \ngreatly scaling back the potential for thunderstorms in lieu of \nmore stratiform rain with the warm front remaining to the south \ninstead of snapping northward into the Upper Midwest. This will \nlimit omega lift and PVA in favor of greater isentropic lift \nacross our coverage area and also tamp down temperatures from \ngetting too far greater than normal. Highs Thursday-Saturday \nwill remain in the 50s to lower 60s, followed by highs Sunday-\nMonday in the upper 50s to mid 60s as this system moves across \nthe region. Behind it, highs will return to the 50s for the \nmiddle of next week along with less active weather.",
